ess","api_error_status":null,"result":"**Undistributed (excess) reportable income**\n- Excess reportable income is the fund's reportable income minus actual distributions. It is **deemed distributed** to you on the fund's \"fund distribution date,\" which is six months after the reporting period ends. You are taxed in the **UK tax year containing that date**, even though you received no cash.\n- It is generally taxed as a **dividend**. If the fund holds more than 60% interest-bearing assets throughout the period (the qualifying investments test), it is taxed as **interest (savings income)** instead.\n- The amount is **added to your CGT base cost**, so it is not taxed twice on disposal.\n\n**Disposal gains**\n- Gains are subject to **capital gains tax**, not income tax. The current rates are 18% and 24%, and the £3,000 annual exempt amount and losses can offset gains.\n- Calculate the gain as proceeds minus cost, where cost includes the reported excess income. This assumes reporting status held throughout ownership.\n\nCheck each fund's annual report (on its website or HMRC's list) and report the figures on your Self Assessment return.","ttft_ms":2526,"type":"result","duration_ms":6052,"uuid":"74fb4f0f-541f-4967-95f9-59aa5c92d59b","ttft_stream_ms":595,"time_to_request_ms":62,"first_content_frame_ms":595,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
